WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.019
I take a look at
a new networking

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:06.840
algorithm that gives your
clients a big performance win.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.490
Is this tech too
good to be true?

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.480
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:14.130
If you follow along
with our content,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.692
you probably know that we never
advocate for a silver bullet

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:18.900
when it comes to performance.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.665
There's always a
horde of trade-offs

00:00:20.665 --> 00:00:22.290
that you have to
consider before things

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.090
make sense for your scenario.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.790
But I'm going to break
my rule this time

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.940
and say that you should really
be using a new networking

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.150
algorithm called TCP
BBR, which can give

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.370
your clients in poor
connectivity areas

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.860
as much as a 2x
boost in performance.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.860
And all you have to do is set
a simple server side flag.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.340
See, TCP as a protocol
tries its best

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.040
to balance between being
fast and being fair,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.460
with much more emphasis
on the being fair part.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.100
For example, as packet loss
on your network increases,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.020
TCP's backoff
algorithm will reduce

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.300
the performance of each
user so that everyone

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.320
can share the pipe.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.010
But fine tuning this
balance is really tricky.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.260
With TCP's current
backoff algorithms,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.390
even if you're the only
person using the pipe,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.090
it can underutilize your network
connection by as much as 50%.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.390
This is where TCP BBR comes in.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.490
It's an updated
backoff algorithm

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.310
that's designed to respond
to actual congestion

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.500
rather than just
simple packet loss,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.020
which means that it's
focused on improving network

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.180
performance in the worst
case scenario, when

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.800
your network isn't very good.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.140
Now sadly, I don't have access
to a consistently unstable

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:29.640
network.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.140
So for the sake
of our test, we're

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.570
going to simulate some
worst case scenarios.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.040
To test things, I set up two VMS
in two different regions, one

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.790
to be the client and
one to be the server,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.870
and did a quick iPerf test to
check out their performance.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.030
Then, to simulate
our unstable network,

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.710
we run the following TC
command on the client,

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.700
which will reduce performance
by increasing packet loss.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.700
Rerunning the test, we see
that this has a dramatic impact

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.639
on our performance.

00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:56.430
Now that we know what
our performance looks

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.810
like in the
problematic scenario,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.270
we enable BBR on
the server, and we

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.380
see that our performance
more than doubles.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.420
And to be clear, we only have
to set this flag on the server.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.699
TCP BBR doesn't
require the client

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:11.580
to have the same BBR
implementation running,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.220
meaning that your legacy clients
can get this performance boost

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.960
without needing an update.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.490
Not bad for a day's work.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.710
If you'd like to know
more details about BBR,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.290
check out the related article
linked in the description

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:22.791
below.

00:02:22.791 --> 00:02:24.914
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:24.914 --> 00:02:26.580
Performance Atlas
content because when

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.340
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

